virglrenderer: Venus SPIR-V is scanned before the Mac's shader compilers

What: vkCreateShaderModule and SPIR-V chained into a pipeline stage go
through a structural scan (src/venus/vkr_spirv.c, no driver): the header
(magic, version up to 1.6, an id bound up to 2^20, schema 0), every
instruction inside the code, the ids the scan reads inside the bound, entry
point names that end. A pipeline then needs its stage's entry point by name
and execution model, a compute workgroup size after specialization
(LocalSize, LocalSizeId or the WorkgroupSize built-in, spec constants
resolved) inside the device's limits, and every descriptor set and binding
that the functions its entry point reaches use in its layout. Descriptors no
function uses need not be there (glslang lists them in SPIR-V 1.4+
interfaces too). A refusal ends the app's context. The scan also keeps what
virgl-venus-spirv-validate.patch checks before spirv-val and at pipeline
creation: the types of Function and Private variables (their elements after
specialization), of Workgroup variables (their bytes, Block ones aliasing
each other), the Input and Output variables with their Location, BuiltIn,
Patch and per-vertex decorations and each entry point's interface (their
locations against the device's limits), and what the types would cost
spirv-val's walks over them (each instruction the weights of the types it
names and reads). An array length that is an OpSpecConstantOp (glslang's
float a[WMITER * TM] or shared x[gl_WorkGroupSize.x * 2], as in ggml's Vulkan
shaders) is evaluated from the int and bool constants, constant composites
and the earlier operations, after the stage's specialization (3.4.5, #624).
The value is the bits masked to the type's width, the S operations
sign-extend and the U ones zero-extend their operands. Where drivers may
disagree or SPIR-V gives no value, the length is invalid: a wrap or
truncation, a divisor of 0, SDiv, SRem or SMod on a negative operand, a
shift past the width, a bool spec value other than 0 or 1, a map entry
whose size is not the constant's, an operand defined at or after the
operation, mixed types, or a length of 0 or less. VectorShuffle,
CompositeInsert, QuantizeToF16, vectors, floats and a 64-bit SpecId are not
evaluated: such a length is refused (Workgroup and interface ones counted
once before; none of the 1267 modules of llama.cpp b11791's ggml-vulkan or
the CPU test's corpus has one). Operations are kept without refusing the
module and evaluated in the order of their words, so there is no recursion
and no loop.
Why: KosmicKrisp's spirv_to_nir ended QEMU on a bad magic (NULL in its own
error path) and on an id bound of 0x3fffffff (SIGBUS in
linear_zalloc_child_array); MoltenVK's SPIRV-Cross on garbage, truncated code
and that bound (SIGSEGV) and with an uncaught exception on a missing entry
point or a stage of another model (SIGABRT); Metal ended MoltenVK on a
workgroup of 65536 threads; KosmicKrisp dereferenced a descriptor set the
layout lacks (kk_nir_lower_descriptors). From 3.2.21 every Function array
whose length was a spec constant operation was refused, so ggml's Vulkan
backend (llama.cpp, whisper.cpp, Superwhisper's workers) lost its context
when it created such a pipeline (#624: hadiskeini, Fail-Safe, dandomin,
epicoun); a Workgroup one counted once, so an array past the device's limit
could pass.
Upstream: OmacVM policy (spec VUIDs), draft only.
Tests: Tests/virgl/fuzz-venus-spirv.sh (the scan alone on the CPU, under
AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, with the element and byte
counts, a glslc seed with ggml's spec constant operations, and chains of
100000 and doubling ones 60 deep); Tests/virgl/test-venus-spirv-validate.c
(each operation, the wraps, divisors of 0, signedness by opcode, map entry
sizes, bools, forward references and cycles); Tests/virgl/
test-venus-commands.c (p-spirv-, p-entry-missing, p-stage-mismatch,
p-shader-layout-, p-unused-set-, p-workgroup- cases).

